    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that rejects creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by another owner, responding with **409 Conflict**. I followed the existing duplicate-telephone pattern so each piece of logic lives where it belongs:\n\n1. **`DuplicateEmailException`** (new, in `rest/validation/`) \u2014 mirrors `DuplicateTelephoneException`, carrying the offending value and exposing the `\"email\"` field name.\n\n2. **Repository layer** \u2014 added `findByEmailIgnoreCase(String)` to the `OwnerRepository` interface and all three implementations (Spring Data JPA, JPA, JDBC), each doing a case-insensitive (`LOWER(...)`) match, consistent with the existing `findByLastNameIgnoreCase`.\n\n3. **Service layer** \u2014 added `findOwnersByEmailIgnoreCase(String)` to `ClinicService` and `ClinicServiceImpl` (read-only transactional), delegating to the repository.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after normalizing the email, it checks (only when non-null) whether any owner already uses that lower-cased email and throws `DuplicateEmailException` if so, before saving.\n\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added a handler that maps `DuplicateEmailException` to a 409 Conflict whose `errors` array names the `email` field, exactly like the sibling duplicate handlers.\n\nThe email normalizer already lower-cases and returns `null` for absent emails, so the check naturally skips owners without an email and compares on the canonical lower-cased form.",
